A detector is written against a stream of sign-in events, and the three decisions that shape it are shown below.

Code sample (written for this course):

// a rule as it would be written against a sign-in event stream
const rule = {
  match:  (e) => e.event === "signin-failed",   // which records count
  groupBy: (e) => e.username,                      // what the count is kept per
  count:   6,                                      // how many count
  within:  "10m",                                 // over what period
};

for (const e of stream) {
  const k = rule.groupBy(e);
  seen[k] = (seen[k] || 0) + 1;
  if (rule.match(e) && seen[k] >= rule.count) raise(k, rule);
}

The rule above is the shape most detectors take. What is it actually keying on, and what sets the limit of what it can find?

Two candidate checks for a request path are shown, and the second one is the team's proposed repair.

Code sample (written for this course):

// the rule, as written by the team
if (request.path.includes("../")) block(request);

// what the rule was tested against
check("../../etc/notes");   // refused
check("..\\etc\\notes");    // allowed through, and reaches the same file

// the fix: decide on the resolved location rather than on the characters
const full = path.resolve(serveRoot, request.path);
if (!full.startsWith(path.resolve(serveRoot) + path.sep)) block(request);

The first check is refused and the second is allowed through, yet both name the same file. What does the pair show about the rule?

A published policy and the code that grants access are shown side by side, and they disagree on every point.

Code sample (written for this course):

// the policy, published on the intranet, approved last year
// "Access to the production database is granted per request,
//  names an approver, and expires after eight hours."

// what the grant service actually does
const grant = await grants.create({
  user: requester,
  scope: "production-db",
  approver: null,
  expiresIn: null,
});

// and how it is granted
await access.allow(grant);

The policy above and the code above disagree on every point. What makes a policy real, and what does this code show about it?

A capture routine and a later verification routine are shown, and both were run over a throwaway tree.

Code sample (written for this course):

// written at the moment of capture, over the files in a fixed order
const chain = [];
let prev = "0".repeat(64);
for (const f of files) {
  const body = sha256(read(f));
  const link = sha256(prev + body);
  chain.push({ file: f, body, link });
  prev = link;
}
write("manifest.json", chain);

// verification, run later over the same directory
let p = "0".repeat(64);
for (const { file, body, link } of chain) {
  if (sha256(read(file)) !== body) break;   // item changed, or is gone
  if (sha256(p + body) !== link) break;    // item is not in its recorded place
  p = link;
}

// confirmed on a throwaway tree: editing one file is caught and located at that
// file, deleting one is caught at that file, and two files swapped is caught at
// the second of them. Trimming the last two entries from the manifest is not.

Run over the same directory a week later, which changes does the routine catch, and which does it not?

An incident summary and a database audit log for the same period are shown, and they do not agree.

Code sample (written for this course):

// the summary, drafted from the sign-in records
// "No database was accessed. The activity was limited to signing in
//  and reading mail. No customer data left the environment."

// the database's own audit log, same period
09:58:02  account=svc_deploy  action=select  table=billing_customers  rows=18400
09:58:04  account=svc_deploy  action=select  table=billing_customers  rows=18400
10:02:11  account=root        action=select  table=billing_customers  rows=18400

The summary says no database was accessed. What does the second record do, and what follows for the summary?
